\'97Projects Page\'97\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The projects shown here represent just a small part of our wider experience, ranging from residential alterations, extensions and loft conversions to new-build homes and more complex structural interventions.
\f3\fs24 \

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 If you are considering a project and would like to see examples of relevant experience, please get in touch and we would be happy to share projects of a similar scale or nature.
\f3\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 \cf0 Project 1 - Additional Floor, Lewes\'a0
\f3\fs24 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 \cf0 The complete transformation and extension of an existing mid-century home, including the addition of a new upper storey and extensive alterations to the existing structure.
\f3\fs24 \

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 The project involved the removal of internal load-bearing walls to create a more open plan arrangement, together with a substantial new opening to the rear of the property. New steelwork was designed to support the altered structure and facilitate the architectural layout.
\f3\fs24 \

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 A significant part of the engineering involved adding an entire additional storey over the existing building. The existing structure and foundations were assessed to establish their capacity to support the additional loads, with localised underpinning designed where required.
\f3\fs24 \

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 The works also included a new side extension and the structural integration of the new and existing elements of the property.
\f3\fs24 \

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 Working closely with the wider project team, the structural design allowed the existing building to be substantially reconfigured and enlarged while retaining and making efficient use of the original structure.
\f3\fs24 \

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 Structural works included: additional storey \'b7 foundation assessment \'b7 localised underpinning \'b7 load-bearing wall removals \'b7 structural steelwork \'b7 large rear opening \'b7 side extension \'b7 alterations to existing structure
\f3\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 Project 2 - Loft Conversion, Haywards Heath
\f3\fs24 \
\

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 Pics to be uploaded once the agent send them over.\'a0
\f3\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 \cf0 The refurbishment and structural reconfiguration of an existing home, creating more open living space at ground-floor level while unlocking the potential of the existing roof to form a new habitable storey.
\f3\fs24 \

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 At ground floor, internal load-bearing walls were removed and new structural elements introduced to support the existing building and accommodate the revised architectural layout.
\f3\fs24 \

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 At first-floor level, the existing timber floor structure was assessed and strengthened to accommodate new partitions and the proposed internal arrangement.
\f3\fs24 \

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 The generous proportions of the existing roof allowed the loft conversion to be created within the existing roof form, without the need for a dormer. New steelwork was carefully integrated within the ceiling and floor zone to support the new loft floor, while existing joists and rafters were strengthened to accommodate the increased loads and new rooflight openings.
\f3\fs24 \

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 The result is a substantial increase in usable living space achieved largely within the existing envelope of the property, with the structural interventions designed to be efficient and discreet.
\f3\fs24 \

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 Structural works included: load-bearing wall removals \'b7 structural steelwork \'b7 first-floor strengthening \'b7 loft conversion \'b7 new loft floor \'b7 rafter strengthening \'b7 rooflight openings \'b7 alterations to existing timber structure
\f3\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 Project 3 - New Build, Warninglid
\f3\fs24 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 \cf0 The structural design of a new-build home in Warninglid, combining traditional masonry construction with carefully integrated structural steelwork to accommodate large open-plan spaces and generous roof spans.
\f3\fs24 \

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 The house was designed using traditional cavity wall construction with a load-bearing masonry inner leaf. To the rear of the property, a substantial steel frame was introduced to support the large roof span while allowing the architectural layout to remain open and largely uninterrupted.
\f3\fs24 \

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 Ground conditions and the proximity of established trees were considered carefully as part of the foundation design. A site investigation was undertaken in coordination with a geotechnical engineer, confirming suitable non-shrinkable ground conditions. This allowed conventional trench-fill foundations to be adopted, avoiding the need for a more complex and costly piled solution.
\f3\fs24 \

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 A suspended beam-and-block ground floor was specified to provide separation from the ground below and accommodate potential ground movement. The roof structure was also designed around the architectural arrangement, including the design and strengthening of rafters around new openings.
\f3\fs24 \

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 The result is a robust and efficient structural solution that combines conventional construction with targeted steelwork, while using site-specific ground information to avoid unnecessary complexity and construction cost.
\f3\fs24 \

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 Structural works included: foundation design \'b7 geotechnical coordination \'b7 trench-fill foundations \'b7 load-bearing masonry \'b7 structural steel frame \'b7 steel beams \'b7 lintel design \'b7 beam-and-block ground floor \'b7 roof structure \'b7 rafter design \'b7 structural openings
\f3\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 Project 4 - New Build, Lyme Regis
\f3\fs24 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 \cf0 The structural design of a contemporary new-build home in the countryside near Lyme Regis, combining timber, masonry and reinforced concrete to respond to the architecture and the sloping rural site.
\f3\fs24 \

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 A key part of the brief was the client's desire to use natural materials wherever possible. Timber therefore formed an important part of the primary structure, requiring careful consideration of achievable spans, member depths and the relationship between the structure and the architectural layout.
\f3\fs24 \

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 Rather than simply introducing steelwork to achieve longer spans, the structural arrangement was developed collaboratively with the client to work within the practical limitations of timber. This allowed exposed and concealed timber elements to be incorporated throughout the building while retaining the character and material palette of the original design.
\f3\fs24 \

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 The lower portions of the house incorporate masonry and reinforced concrete construction, with the foundations and reinforced concrete floor structure designed to respond to the site's levels and support the timber structure above.
\f3\fs24 \

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 The result is a structural solution in which the choice of materials forms an integral part of the architecture, balancing structural efficiency with the client's preference for a natural, timber-led building.
\f3\fs24 \

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 Structural works included: foundation design \'b7 timber frame design \'b7 timber beams and roof structure \'b7 masonry design \'b7 reinforced concrete floor design \'b7 structural openings \'b7 coordination of timber spans \'b7 integration of structure with architectural design
\f3\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 Project 5 - New Build, The Writers Cabin, Dorset\'a0\'a0
\f3\fs24 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 \cf0 The structural design of a new-build timber cabin set within the Dorset countryside, developed around a strong architectural ambition to use natural materials and allow the structure to form part of the character of the building.
\f3\fs24 \

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 Timber was used extensively for the primary structural frame, requiring detailed engineering to establish an efficient structural arrangement while retaining the architectural form and material palette envisioned for the project.
\f3\fs24 \

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 Particular attention was given to the analysis of both 
\f4\b gravity and lateral loads
\f1\b0 , with the timber frame designed not only to support the roof and floor loads but also to provide the stability required to resist wind and other horizontal forces. Connections, load paths and the interaction between individual timber elements were carefully considered as part of the overall structural strategy.
\f3\fs24 \

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 At foundation level, reinforced concrete was introduced where its structural properties were most appropriate, providing a robust base from which the timber superstructure could be constructed.
\f3\fs24 \

\f1\fs29\fsmilli14667 The result is a carefully engineered building where the structure and architecture work together, using reinforced concrete where necessary while allowing timber and natural materials to define the character of the finished cabin.
\f3\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f4\b\fs29\fsmilli14667 \cf0 Structural works included:
\f1\b0  reinforced concrete foundation design \'b7 timber frame design \'b7 gravity load analysis \'b7 lateral stability design \'b7 timber beams and roof structure \'b7 structural connections \'b7 load-path development \'b7 structural detailing
\f3\fs24 \
